WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:04.580
There's bound to be a film out there that
pushes one or two of your buttons.

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:08.130
These movies, on the other hand, push every
single one of them.

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:10.309
These movies aren't just hard to watch.

00:00:10.309 --> 00:00:11.990
They aren't just edgy.

00:00:11.990 --> 00:00:16.219
And they don't just challenge the entire concept
of good taste.

00:00:16.220 --> 00:00:19.220
They're deeply disturbing on almost every
level.

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:24.040
Director Eli Roth is no stranger to movies
that are hard to sit through.

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:29.539
His 2006 horror hit Hostel proved that beyond
a shadow of a doubt, and turned his name into

00:00:29.539 --> 00:00:32.289
a synonym for over-the-top violence.

00:00:32.289 --> 00:00:37.680
With The Green Inferno, however, Roth pushes
things further than ever before, and unless

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:41.660
you're a seasoned gorehound, chances are you
won't make it to the end.

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:47.260
In the grand tradition of the video nasties
of the '70s and '80s, Inferno tells the tale

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:52.780
of a group college-aged activists who head
to the Amazon to protest a logging operation.

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:54.280
They find cannibals instead.

00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:58.600
"We are all going to escape tonight, all of
us, I promise."

00:00:58.600 --> 00:00:59.100
"Okay?"

00:00:59.140 --> 00:00:59.640
"Okay."

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:05.920
You can probably imagine the non-stop stream
of gore and misery that follows, and if you

00:01:05.930 --> 00:01:09.240
can't last through the whole movie, well,
that's kind of the point.

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:11.820
In an interview with Rolling Stone, Roth said:

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:16.590
"If I've really done my job as a director,
nobody can actually watch your movie…

00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:21.969
You don't want people walking out of a movie;
you want them running out of the theater screaming.

00:01:21.969 --> 00:01:24.909
When that happens, that's like a standing
ovation for me."

00:01:24.909 --> 00:01:30.630
The Green Inferno achieves its unsettling
dread by shooting on location in Peru, utilizing

00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:36.439
physical special effects, and casting members
of the Amazon's native population in key roles.

00:01:36.439 --> 00:01:41.540
That's led some critics to accuse Inferno
of cultural appropriation, but Roth doesn't

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:42.200
seem to mind.

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:46.640
He's insisted that the amateur actors were
all paid fairly, and while they didn't have

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:51.440
much experience in front of the camera they,
quote, "got it right away and loved it."

00:01:56.660 --> 00:02:02.520
Don't confuse Martyrs, the 2008 French thriller,
with its 2015 American remake.

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:07.420
Both movies have roughly the same plot, but
there's one big difference: the English language

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:12.210
version is remarkably tame, despite its inherently
unsettling subject matter.

00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:13.220
That's by design.

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:15.700
Writer Mark L. Smith told Creative Screenwriting:

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:17.820
"I'm not a lover of violence.

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:22.270
I tried to stay away from all the violence
and keep it offscreen, which was kind of the

00:02:22.270 --> 00:02:24.110
polar opposite of the original."

00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:29.470
So if you've got a low tolerance for abuse
and still want to watch something messed up

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:31.300
for some reason, stick with that one.

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:35.380
On the other hand, if you want to test your
mettle, go for the original.

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:39.970
With graphic depictions of brutal acts, Martyrs
isn't for the faint of heart.

00:02:39.970 --> 00:02:43.700
When it screened at Cannes, audiences left
the theaters in droves.

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:45.400
They just couldn't handle it.

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:50.010
From the basic plot to the film's horrifying
climax it's easy to understand why.

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:55.010
Sure, Martyrs is also a nuanced story about
guilt and friendship, and Smith is right when

00:02:55.010 --> 00:02:57.540
he says that there's more to it than mere
gore.

00:02:57.540 --> 00:02:59.910
But the gore is a big part of it.

00:02:59.910 --> 00:03:03.880
Like the New York Times wrote, this isn't
one for amateurs.

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:08.940
You won't find everything that makes Irreversible
hard to watch on screen.

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:12.570
Don't get us wrong: Emotionally, the movie
is absolutely brutal.

00:03:12.570 --> 00:03:16.790
It's bad enough that even its star, Monica
Bellucci, can't sit through it.

00:03:16.790 --> 00:03:22.540
But that's not the only reason over 250 people
fled the film's Cannes premiere, with many

00:03:22.540 --> 00:03:24.700
fainting or seeking medical treatment.

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:26.960
The sound played a big role, too.

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:33.430
See, Irreversible uses infrasound, or low-frequency
sound waves, to augment its unsettling visuals.

00:03:33.430 --> 00:03:38.130
You can't actually hear the sound, but your
body registers it anyway, leading to feelings

00:03:38.130 --> 00:03:43.600
of anxiety, unease, distress, and depression,
in addition to the shivers and, sometimes,

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:44.100
nausea.

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:46.300
"It's sort of anxiety producing.

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:50.920
I don't know about fear, but it's unnerving."

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:56.660
It's a trick that many modern horror films
use, including Paranormal Activity, and while

00:03:56.670 --> 00:04:01.510
infrasound doesn't affect every member of
the audience in the same way, it's credited

00:04:01.510 --> 00:04:06.230
as one of the reasons why Irreversible makes
many members of its audience feel sick.

00:04:06.230 --> 00:04:10.520
In fact, many people who watch don't even
make it very far into the film.

00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:15.459
Thanks to the soundtrack, a mere half hour
of Irreversible is more than enough for many

00:04:15.459 --> 00:04:20.370
viewers, forcing them to turn the film off
before its most horrific action even truly

00:04:20.370 --> 00:04:20.860
begins.

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:26.140
When The Woman made its Sundance debut, many
people left the theater.

00:04:26.150 --> 00:04:27.980
Others simply wished they had.

00:04:27.980 --> 00:04:32.880
By all indications, that's exactly the type
of reaction director Lucky McKee was hoping

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:33.380
for.

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:38.760
The plot, which centers on a dysfunctional
family's attempt to "civilize" a wild woman

00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:43.759
by locking her in the basement and torturing
her, is explicitly designed to push every

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:46.880
misogynistic and toxically masculine button.

00:04:47.260 --> 00:04:48.640
"Do we really get to keep her?"

00:04:49.100 --> 00:04:49.620
"We do."

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:57.259
Oddly, it's also a thoughtful and nuanced
film, at least once you get past its surface

00:04:57.259 --> 00:04:58.259
shocks.

00:04:58.259 --> 00:04:59.530
It's not all gore and gloom.

00:04:59.530 --> 00:05:01.240
The Woman has something to say.

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:05.650
It also has a pretty decent Rotten Tomatoes
score, especially for this kind of thing.

00:05:05.650 --> 00:05:10.319
There's a rewarding experience lurking underneath
the discomfiting chills, you just have to

00:05:10.319 --> 00:05:12.669
last through the whole movie to find it.

00:05:12.669 --> 00:05:13.160
Good luck.

00:05:13.480 --> 00:05:18.200
Filmgoers at the Toronto International Film
Festival should've known what they were in

00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:18.700
for.

00:05:18.700 --> 00:05:23.800
After all, its late-night "Midnight Madness"
programming block has its name for a reason:

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:25.120
anything goes.

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:31.460
Unfortunately, not everyone got the memo,
and one film fan found Coralie Fargeat's Revenge

00:05:31.460 --> 00:05:35.270
so intense that he had a seizure right in
the middle of the theater.

00:05:35.270 --> 00:05:40.270
Reportedly, it was a stomach churning scene
in which a man is forced to remove a giant

00:05:40.270 --> 00:05:42.669
piece of glass from his foot that did it.

00:05:42.669 --> 00:05:43.840
Fargeat told IndieWire:

00:05:44.060 --> 00:05:47.900
"We started to hear someone say, 'hello, hello,'
from the audience.

00:05:47.910 --> 00:05:52.870
I didn't know if it was someone making [a
joke] in the room, then I see the paramedics

00:05:52.870 --> 00:05:53.870
in the cinema."

00:05:53.870 --> 00:05:55.900
Thankfully, the audience member was fine.

00:05:56.060 --> 00:05:57.440
He was in good company, too.

00:05:57.500 --> 00:06:02.740
Revenge lead Matilda Lutz admitted that the
scene made her feel "weird" as well, and she

00:06:02.840 --> 00:06:04.610
was there when the scene was shot.

00:06:04.610 --> 00:06:08.630
If that scene doesn't get you, the rest of
Revenge very will might.

00:06:08.630 --> 00:06:13.409
Starring Lutz as a woman out for vengeance
after being left for dead by her lover and

00:06:13.409 --> 00:06:18.770
his friends, all of which is shown on screen,
naturally, Revenge is a lean, brutal, and

00:06:18.770 --> 00:06:23.770
extremely well-reviewed thriller that's just
as thrilling as it is disturbing.

00:06:23.770 --> 00:06:25.000
Just don't take it lightly.

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:39.039
Lars von Trier has been down this road before.

00:06:39.039 --> 00:06:44.249
In 2009, the director rolled into Cannes with
Antichrist, a film that probably deserves

00:06:44.249 --> 00:06:46.370
its own entry on this list.

00:06:46.370 --> 00:06:52.240
It's wildly misogynistic, unsettling, and
is relentlessly bleak, and people walked out

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:52.800
when it screened.

00:06:53.740 --> 00:06:58.700
"Chaos reigns."

00:07:02.740 --> 00:07:05.919
A decade later, von Trier did it again.

00:07:05.919 --> 00:07:11.389
After doling out a multi-year ban to von Trier
after the director said he sympathized with

00:07:11.389 --> 00:07:16.309
Adolf Hitler, Cannes authorities decided to
let the filmmaker exhibit his latest picture

00:07:16.309 --> 00:07:17.840
at the 2018 festival.

00:07:18.020 --> 00:07:19.140
Well, surprise!

00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:23.560
The House that Jack Built is even more difficult
to stomach than its predecessor.

00:07:23.560 --> 00:07:28.139
In the movie, Matt Dillon plays a serial killer
on a 12-year murder spree.

00:07:28.139 --> 00:07:31.030
When The House that Jack Built screened, people
left.

00:07:31.030 --> 00:07:32.749
Not just one or two, either.

00:07:32.749 --> 00:07:37.460
Reportedly, more than 100 festival guests
decided to leave the theater rather than finish

00:07:37.460 --> 00:07:38.460
watching.

00:07:38.460 --> 00:07:42.930
In the aftermath, attendees turned to Twitter
to express their disgust, calling von Trier's

00:07:42.930 --> 00:07:47.809
movie "vomitive" and claiming that it, quote,
"should not have been made."

00:07:47.809 --> 00:07:50.289
Fair criticisms, but they should've expected
it.

00:07:50.289 --> 00:07:54.649
The House that Jack Built is so gross that
it wasn't even allowed to compete for the

00:07:54.649 --> 00:07:57.360
Palme d'Or, Cannes' top prize.

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:03.120
Instead, it screened outside of competition
in order to avoid any Antichrist-like controversies.

00:08:03.780 --> 00:08:08.740
When horror fans entered the theater to see
Bite at Fantasia Fest 2015, they received

00:08:08.749 --> 00:08:11.120
special Bite-branded barf bags.

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:12.449
It wasn't a joke.

00:08:12.449 --> 00:08:17.930
As Fantasia Fest co-director Mitch Davis posted
on Facebook, during the screening, at least

00:08:17.930 --> 00:08:19.379
two people passed out.

00:08:19.379 --> 00:08:20.770
One hit his head on the stairs.

00:08:20.770 --> 00:08:22.029
Another started puking.

00:08:22.029 --> 00:08:27.020
By the time the film wrapped, an ambulance
was on site, treating various members of the

00:08:27.020 --> 00:08:28.740
audience for illness.

00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:34.190
That's a pretty strong reaction, but on the
other hand, Bite is a particularly gross movie.

00:08:34.190 --> 00:08:40.240
The horror begins in Costa Rica, where a bride-to-be
receives a mysterious insect bite while celebrating

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:41.240
her bachelorette party.

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:42.280
"I'm fine.

00:08:42.280 --> 00:08:44.300
It's just a small bug bite."

00:08:45.220 --> 00:08:47.840
Not that Casey has time to worry about it,
of course.

00:08:47.840 --> 00:08:51.950
She's already struggling with her upcoming
wedding, her domineering soon-to-be mother-in-law,

00:08:51.950 --> 00:08:55.610
and her fiancé's child filled plans for their
future.

00:08:55.610 --> 00:09:00.950
Like Bite's audience, however, Casey doesn't
realize the severity of her situation.

00:09:00.950 --> 00:09:05.850
Before long she's puking up pus, laying egg
sacks around her apartment, raising a hive

00:09:05.850 --> 00:09:11.810
of carnivorous monsters, and watching as her
body decomposes, revealing the insectoid form

00:09:11.810 --> 00:09:14.650
underneath.

00:09:14.650 --> 00:09:28.030
In other words, Bite quickly goes from a middling
character drama into full-on body horror,

00:09:28.030 --> 00:09:31.850
and it doesn't really offer a break once it
kicks into high gear.

00:09:31.850 --> 00:09:35.380
And as Fantasia Fest proves, it's not an easy
movie to finish.

00:09:35.380 --> 00:09:40.000
In fact, you may find it to be something of
an endurance test if you do sit down to watch

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:40.500
it.

00:09:40.500 --> 00:09:44.340
When a filmmaker says their movie is going
to make you vomit, believe them.

00:09:44.340 --> 00:09:46.640
It's far, far better than the alternative.

00:09:51.320 --> 00:09:57.160
Sam Peckinpah's grim and gritty western The
Wild Bunch might be considered a classic now,

00:09:57.160 --> 00:10:02.680
but during its initial run, the movie's hardcore
violence was too much for late '60s moviegoers.

00:10:02.680 --> 00:10:07.190
Preview audiences lambasted the film, calling
it "wasted insanity."

00:10:07.190 --> 00:10:12.750
After launch, cowboy king John Wayne complained
that The Wild Bunch's blood-soaked action,

00:10:12.750 --> 00:10:15.660
quote, "destroyed the myth of the Old West."

00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:25.220
Even by modern standards, The Wild Bunch is
still grotesquely violent.

00:10:25.220 --> 00:10:29.700
In the '90s, Warner Bros. tried to release
a director's cut with 10 extra minutes of

00:10:29.700 --> 00:10:30.700
footage.

00:10:30.700 --> 00:10:35.810
None of that new stuff was particularly gory,
but the MPAA still took the opportunity to

00:10:35.810 --> 00:10:41.780
change The Wild Bunch's rating from an R to
an NC-17, keeping the film out of theaters

00:10:41.780 --> 00:10:43.960
for an extra two years.

00:10:43.960 --> 00:10:49.780
The Wild Bunch is brutal and uncompromising
and, at the time, was too much for many theater

00:10:49.780 --> 00:10:54.280
attendees, some of whom walked out just 20
minutes into the film.

00:10:54.280 --> 00:10:58.420
Just because The Wild Bunch is a classic doesn't
mean it's easy to watch.

00:10:58.420 --> 00:11:03.380
Even today, all of that bloody ultra-violence
means that you might have to step away before

00:11:03.380 --> 00:11:04.910
the final credits roll.

00:11:04.910 --> 00:11:06.170
Don't be ashamed.

00:11:06.170 --> 00:11:07.480
You'd hardly be the first one.

00:11:07.620 --> 00:11:11.160
"Ain't like it used to be, but, uh … it'll
do."

